<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="chp10sec1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Concepts of Invariant Lines</title>

  <p>
    Processes are everywhere.  
    We want to develop a deep understanding of processes.  
    Chaining together processes gave rise to the notion of <em>composition</em>.  
    Reversing a process gave rise to the notion of <em>inverses</em>.
    Our next fundamental idea associated to a process is <em>invariance</em>.  
  </p>

  <p>
    The the core idea of invariance is that something does not change after having performed a process.  Rotations in the plane do not change length.  Length is an <em>invariant</em> of rotation.  Shears in the plane do not change area.  Area is an <em>invariant</em> of shears.  
  </p>

  <p>
    Recall that if <m>L\subset \R^n</m> is a line and <m>T:\R^n\to \R^n</m> is a matrix transformation, then <m>T(L)</m> is also a line (<xref ref="prop-matrices_transform_lines"/>).
    We will now compare the lines <m>L</m> and <m>T(L)</m>.  
    Might there be some lines for which <m>L</m> and <m>T(L)</m> are the same?
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-invariant_lines">
    <title>Invariant Lines</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>T:\R^n\to \R^n</m> is a matrix transformation with associated matrix <m>A</m>, then a line <m>L</m> through the origin (a.k.a. a 1-dimensional subspace) is <term><m>T</m>-invariant</term> (or <term><m>A</m>-invariant</term>) if it is sent into itself under the transformation.  Symbolically, <m>T(L)\subset L</m>.  When <m>T</m> is clear from context, we will usually drop reference to <m>A</m> or <m>T</m> and just say the line is <term>invariant</term>.  
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Write the line <m>L=\{t\vect{x} \ | \ t\in \R\}</m> in parametric vector form (<xref ref="def-line"/>) for some nonzero vector <m>\vect{x}\in L</m>.
    If <m>L</m> is <m>T</m>-invariant, then <m>T</m> must send <m>\vect{x}</m> to some other point on the line, i.e. <m>T(\vect{x})=\lambda \vect{x}</m> for some <m>\lambda\in \R</m>.
    It follows from the scaling property if matrix multiplication (<xref ref="prop_fundamental_matrix_mult_props"/>),that <m>T</m> then scales <em>each</em> point on the line by this same <m>\lambda</m>.
  </p>

  <definition xml:id="def-associated_scaling_factor">
    <title>Associated Scaling Factor</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>T:\R^n\to \R^n</m> is a matrix transformation and <m>L=\spn(\vect{x})</m> is a <m>T</m>-invariant line, then the value <m>\lambda\in \R</m> for which <m>T(\vect{x})=\lambda \vect{x}</m> is the  <term>associated scaling factor</term>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    Understanding <m>T</m>-invariant lines and their associated scaling factors gives us an incredible about of information about <m>T</m>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some questions to start our investigations:
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li><p><em>How do we find invariant lines?</em></p></li>
    <li><p><em>How many invariant lines does each elementary geometric matrix transformation have?</em></p></li>
    <li><p><em>How does knowing invariant lines help us better understand the transformation?</em></p></li>
    <li><p><em>How might matrix decompositions help us better understand invariant lines?</em></p></li>
    <li><p><em>How might we generalize this?</em></p></li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    What else might you wonder about invariant lines as it relates to linear algebra?
  </p>

  <p>
    Let's set out to find these lines and scaling factors in the plane.  We shall do this concretely, using GeoGebra to explore.
  </p>

  <activity>
	<title>The Invariant Line Finder</title>
	<p>
		In this creation activity, you will create The Invariant Line Finder Tool.
	</p>

	<ol>
		<li>
		<p><alert>Creating your matrix.</alert></p>
		<p>
			Input a matrix <m>A</m> with slider entries, and for the moment, slide the entries so your matrix is 
			<m>A=\left[ \begin{array}{rr}2.5 \amp -0.5 \\ -0.5 \amp 2.5 \end{array}\right]</m>.
		</p>
		</li>
		<li>
		<p><alert>Creating your line and transformed line.</alert></p>
		<p>
			Create the point <m>O=(0,0)</m> and another moveable point <m>B</m>.
			Create the line between <m>O</m> and <m>B</m>.
			ApplyMatrix to <m>B</m>.  It will probably be called <m>B'</m>.
			Create the line between <m>O</m> and <m>B'</m>.  
			(Challenge:  Create the text ``INVARIANT!!!'' and make it appear only on the condition that the line <m>OB</m> equals the line <m>OB'</m>.)
		</p>
		</li>
	</ol>
</activity>

  <p>
    Once completed, your GeoGebra activity should look like GeoGebra ID:
    <url href="https://www.geogebra.org/m/sjxcdtxh"><alert>sjxcdtxh</alert></url>.
  </p>

  <question xml:id="geogebra-invariant_lines">
    <title>Finding and Analyzing Invariant Lines</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        In this activity, you will use your The Invariant Line Finder Tool to find and analyze invariant lines and their associated scaling factors.
        You will discover how different matrices have different numbers of invariant lines.
        In certain situations, you will find similar matrix decompositions.
      </p>

      <p>
        Start with <m>A=\left[ \begin{array}{rr}2.5 \amp -0.5 \\ -0.5 \amp 2.5 \end{array}\right]</m>.
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p><alert>Finding invariant lines and scaling factors.</alert></p>
          <p>
            Explore.  Move your point <m>B</m> and find all invariant lines. 
            (Pro Tip: Try moving <m>B</m> to grid points, where GeoGebra can lock in values.)
            For each invariant line, find the associated scaling factor.  
            First, can you visually see the scaling factor?  
            After approximating visually, you can compute the stretch factor exactly by analyzing the ratio of the length of <m>B'</m> to the length of <m>B</m>.  
            Does the scaling factor include a reflection?
            If you can find two distinct invariant lines, find a similar matrix decomposition for <m>A</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><alert>Varying the matrix - Part 1.</alert></p>
          <p>
            Consider our geometric transformations of the plane: uniform stretches, stretches, reflections, shears, rotations, and projections.  How many distinct lines are invariant?  What is the most (resp. least) number of lines that can be invariant?
          </p>
          <p>
            For example, analyze each of the following:
          </p>
          <me>  
            \left[ \begin{array}{rr}2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 2 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
            \left[ \begin{array}{rr}2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
            \left[ \begin{array}{rr}-1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
            \left[ \begin{array}{rr}0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
            \left[ \begin{array}{rr}2 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 2 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
            \left[ \begin{array}{rr}3 \amp -4 \\ 4 \amp 3 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
          </me>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p><alert>Varying the matrix - Part 2.</alert></p>
          <p>
            Repeat your above analysis for each of the following matrices (i.e. find invariant lines, find scaling factors, and find similar matrix decomposition):
          </p>
          <me>  
            M_1 = \left[ \begin{array}{rr}1.25 \amp 2.25 \\ -0.25 \amp 2.75 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
            M_2 = \left[ \begin{array}{rr}-0.4 \amp -1.2 \\ -1.2 \amp 1.4 \end{array}\right] \hspace{2pc}
            M_3 = \left[ \begin{array}{rr}2 \amp -2.5 \\ 10 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]
          </me>
          <p>
            Explain how knowing the different number of invariant lines a matrix has tells you something about the nature of the associated transformation (e.g. stretch vs. shear vs... etc).
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </question>

  <p>
    Upon reflecting on GeoGebra Activity <xref ref="geogebra-invariant_lines"/>, you should make the following observations: 
  </p>

  <p>
    <alert>Observation 1:</alert>  Different transformations have different numbers of invariant lines:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><p>All lines are invariant under a uniform stretch.</p></li>
    <li><p>Two lines are invariant under stretches and reflections.</p></li>
    <li><p>One line is invariant under a shear.</p></li>
    <li><p>No lines are invariant under rotations.</p></li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    <alert>Observation 2:</alert>  The invariant line(s) for the standard geometric transformation given by:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li><p><m>\begin{bmatrix}a \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp d\end{bmatrix}</m> are the <m>x</m>-axis and <m>y</m>-axis,</p></li>
    <li><p><m>\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp b \\ 0 \amp 1\end{bmatrix}</m> is the <m>x</m>-axis, and</p></li>
    <li><p><m>\begin{bmatrix}1 \amp 0 \\ c \amp 1\end{bmatrix}</m> is the <m>y</m>-axis.</p></li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    <alert>Observation 3:</alert>  If you have enough invariant lines, you can build a similar matrix decomposition for your matrix!
  </p>

  <question>
    <title>Using SMD to find invariant Lines</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If you have a nice similar matrix decomposition, you can quickly read off its invariant lines.  
        For each of the following matrices, determine its invariant lines and associated scaling factors.
      </p>
      <me>
        A=\left[ \begin{array}{rr}1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}2 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 3 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}1 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]^{-1}
        \hspace{2pc}
        B=\left[ \begin{array}{rr}3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}1 \amp 1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]^{-1}
      </me>
      <me>
        C=\left[ \begin{array}{rr}2 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}1 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}2 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]^{-1}
        \hspace{2pc}
        D=\left[ \begin{array}{rr}3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}0 \amp -1 \\ 1 \amp 0 \end{array}\right]\left[ \begin{array}{rr}3 \amp 1 \\ 1 \amp 2 \end{array}\right]^{-1}
      </me>
    </statement>
  </question>

  <p>
    In fact, this is more generally true.  Try to use Definition <xref ref="def-invariant_lines"/> to justify each of the following propositions.
  </p>

  <proposition xml:id="prop-invariant_lines_smd">
    <title>Invariant Lines and SMDs</title>
	<statement>
		<p>
			    If <m>\mathcal{B}= \{\vect{v}_1,\ldots, \vect{v}_n\}\subset \R^n</m> is a basis,
    <m>P=[\vect{v}_1|\cdots| \vect{v}_n]</m>, 
    <m>D</m> is a diagonal matrix with diagonal entries <m>\lambda_1,\ldots, \lambda_n</m>, and
    <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>, 
    then for each <m>i</m>, <m>1\le i\le n</m>, the line <m>L_i = \{t \vect{v}_i \ | \ t\in \mathbb{R}\}</m> is an <m>A</m>-invariant line with scaling factor <m>\lambda_i</m>.
		</p>
	</statement>
  </proposition>

  <proof>
    <p>
      It suffices to show that <m>A\vect{v}_i=\lambda_i\vect{v}_i</m>.  
    </p>
    <me>
      A\vect{v}_i =P(D(P^{-1}\vect{v}_i))=P(D\vect{e}_i)=P(\lambda_i\vect{e}_i)=\lambda_i(P\vect{e}_i)=\lambda_i\vect{v}_i.
    </me>
  </proof>

  <definition xml:id="def-invariant_subspace">
    <title>Invariant Subspaces</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>T:\R^n\to \R^n</m> is a matrix transformation with associated matrix <m>A</m>, then a subspace <m>W\subset \R^n</m> is <term><m>T</m>-invariant</term> (or <term><m>A</m>-invariant</term>) if <m>T(W)\subset W</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    We may generalize <xref ref="prop-invariant_lines_smd"/> to any <m>T</m>-invariant subspace.
  </p>

  <p>
    <alert>Invariant Subspaces and SMDs</alert>
    If <m>W</m> is an invariant subspace of <m>B</m> and <m>A=PBP^{-1}</m>, 
    then <m>P(W)</m> is an invariant subspace of <m>A</m>.
  </p>

  <example>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that <m>S=\{\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_3\}\subset \R^3</m> is an ordered basis.  The <m>3\times 3</m> matrix 
      </p>
      <me>
        A=[\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_3]\begin{bmatrix}2 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp -1 \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 0\end{bmatrix}[\vect{v}_1,\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_3]^{-1}.
      </me>
      <p>
        We can see two invariant subspaces.  The line <m>L=\spn(\vect{v}_1)</m> is <m>A</m>-invariant with scaling factor 2.  The plane <m>W=\spn(\vect{v}_2,\vect{v}_3)</m> is invariant and <m>T_A</m> acts upon it by a rotation of <m>90^\circ</m> <m>\vect{v}_2</m> towards <m>\vect{v}_3</m>.  
      </p>
    </statement>
  </example>



</section>